<Base title="About" description="About Talha M Blog: an independent news website explaining world, business, celebrity, sports, AI and Pakistan stories in simple English, run by founder and writer Talha Mehmood.">
  <section class="article-head"><h1>About</h1></section>
  <div class="prose">
<p><em>Last updated: October 16, 2026 — 11:53 PM (Pakistan time)</em></p>
    <p>Welcome to Talha M Blog — a news and information website where stories from around the world are explained in simple, clear English. Many people read the news in a second or third language, and hard words should never stand between a reader and the truth of a story. That is why this website exists: students, busy workers, freelancers and ordinary readers can understand what happened, without struggling with difficult language.</p>
    <p>Every story here tries to answer a few simple questions honestly: What happened? When and where did it happen? Who is involved? And why does it matter to ordinary people? We give the background as well as the latest update, in our own words.</p>

    <h2>What we write about</h2>
    <p>Our stories are organised by their nature into these categories:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>AI:</b> new AI tools, AI companies and artificial-intelligence news.</li>
      <li><b>Nature:</b> remarkable places, animals, weather wonders and the natural world.</li>
      <li><b>Business:</b> companies, markets, jobs, money stories and people in business.</li>
      <li><b>Conflict:</b> wars, attacks and tensions between countries, reported carefully.</li>
      <li><b>Sports:</b> cricket, football and other sports news, records and stories.</li>
      <li><b>Celebrity:</b> news and full life stories about actors, singers, athletes and other public figures.</li>
      <li><b>US News:</b> important stories from the United States.</li>
      <li><b>Crime:</b> major crime and court stories, with allegations described as allegations until a court decides.</li>
      <li><b>Disasters:</b> earthquakes, floods, storms, accidents and rescue efforts.</li>
      <li><b>Pakistan News:</b> politics, government decisions and important events inside Pakistan.</li>
      <li><b>Awards:</b> major prizes, honours and the people who receive them.</li>
      <li><b>Health:</b> health and public-health news — for information only, never as medical advice (see our <a href="/disclaimer/">Disclaimer</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Politics:</b> governments, elections and major political decisions, explained without taking sides.</li>
      <li><b>Technology:</b> phones, apps, companies and everyday technology.</li>
      <li><b>Space:</b> rockets, space stations, planets and space missions.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Most of our stories are international, and we also cover Pakistan. A story is chosen because it is important, interesting or useful for ordinary readers — never because someone paid for it to appear.</p>

    <h2>How we prepare a story</h2>
    <p>Before a story is published, we check the facts against reliable news sources. For important stories, we compare reports from at least three independent sources, because a single report can be wrong, incomplete, or later corrected by the people involved. We write in our own words; we do not copy and paste other websites' articles, and we do not present rumours as confirmed news. When a report is not confirmed, we say so inside the story.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Every post shows the date and time it was published, in Pakistan time (PKT).</li>
      <li>When a story develops, we update the post, and the page shows an "Updated" date.</li>
      <li>A green dot beside a post means it was published in the last 48 hours.</li>
      <li>Our stories name the sources they are based on, and link to the original reports, so you can read them yourself.</li>
      <li>Like many modern publishers, we sometimes use AI writing tools to help prepare drafts. Every story is checked against reliable sources and edited before it is published, and the editor of this website is responsible for everything that appears here.</li>
      <li>The pictures on this website are illustrations made for our stories, so readers should not mistake them for real photographs of the people or events described. In the rare case that a real photograph from another source is ever used, it will be credited on the page.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Ways to read and join in</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Comments:</b> You can comment under stories with a name and an email address. There is no account and no password, your email address is never shown publicly, and comments appear immediately. The rules for comments are in our <a href="/terms/">Terms of Use</a>, and how comment information is handled is explained in our <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Newsletter:</b> You can subscribe free on story pages to receive new stories by email. The newsletter is run through Kit. You must confirm your email address first (double opt-in), and every email carries an unsubscribe link.</li>
      <li><b>Search:</b> The search box looks through this website's own stories by name, place or topic. It works only inside our own pages.</li>
      <li><b>Install the app:</b> Talha M Blog can be installed on your phone or computer from the browser, like an app. Installing is optional and free, and it helps pages open faster.</li>
      <li><b>Author page:</b> Visit our <a href="/author/">Author page</a> to meet the writer and browse every published story.</li>
      <li><b>Light and dark modes:</b> You can switch the reading mode; your choice is remembered in your own browser.</li>
      <li><b>Share buttons:</b> Stories can be shared to WhatsApp, Facebook, X and Telegram, or by copying the link.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Our promise about honesty</h2>
    <p>Trust matters more than traffic. We do not invent quotations, and we do not make up numbers, statistics, traffic figures or earnings. If we do not know something, we say so. If a viral claim is exaggerated, we explain what is actually known — even when the truth is less exciting than the rumour.</p>

    <h2>Corrections and right of reply</h2>
    <p>Mistakes can happen, especially when news changes quickly. When we find an error ourselves, or a reader reports one that checks out, we correct the post and mark it as updated. If a story is about you or your organisation and you believe something in it is wrong or unfair, email us at contact@talhablog.com with the story link and your response. Where it is fair, we will add your response or correct the story. Our <a href="/contact/">Contact page</a> explains how to reach us for corrections and other matters.</p>

    <h2>Who runs Talha M Blog?</h2>
    <p>Talha M Blog is run by Talha Mehmood — its founder, writer and editor. It is a small, independent website from Pakistan, started in October 2026. It is not part of a large media group, a political party or any company. One person chooses the topics, checks the facts, writes the stories and answers the email.</p>

    <h2>Advertising and independence</h2>
    <p>We have applied to support this website with advertising served by Google AdSense, which is described in our <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>. Our application is under review, and no advertisements are shown yet. Once Google approves our application, advertisements will appear on our pages. Advertisements never decide what we write, and no advertiser can pay to change, hide or remove a story. Visitors from Europe and some US states are shown a consent message about advertising cookies, and every reader can manage their choices as explained in the Privacy Policy.</p>

    <h2>Why simple English?</h2>
    <p>News is often published in English first, even though many readers speak another language at home. A student preparing for exams, a freelancer working with foreign clients, or a parent reading the news at night may all be reading in a language that is not their first. Writing simply means explaining with care — it does not mean writing less, or hiding the difficult parts of a story.</p>

    <h2>Follow and contact us</h2>
    <p>Follow Talha M Blog on Instagram at <a href="https://www.instagram.com/talh_ablog" rel="noopener" target="_blank">@talh_ablog</a> for our newest stories. For questions, feedback, suggestions or corrections, email us at contact@talhablog.com or visit our <a href="/contact/">Contact page</a>. We welcome genuine messages and corrections.</p>

